 !!! Note
     The minimum last stake amount can be configured using `last_stake_amount_min_ratio` - which defaults to 0.5 (50%). This means that the minimum stake amount that's ever used is `stake_amount * 0.5`. This avoids very low stake amounts, that are close to the minimum tradable amount for the pair and can be refused by the exchange.
 
+#### Maximum currency exposure
+
+If you trade several pairs that are all priced against, or backed by, the same underlying currency (for example several BTC-denominated pairs, or several coins quoted in the same stablecoin), a large adverse move in that one currency can hit several of your open trades at once. `max_currency_exposure` limits the concentration risk from this by capping the share of your tradable capital that may be committed, at any one time, to trades whose pair shares the same underlying (base) currency.
+
+For example, with a tradable balance of 1000 USDT and `max_currency_exposure=0.3`, no more than 300 USDT may be committed at once to trades on pairs whose base currency is BTC (e.g. `BTC/USDT`), independently of any limit applying to trades on other base currencies (e.g. `ETH/USDT`).
+
+When opening (or increasing the size of) a trade would push the combined stake for its underlying currency beyond the configured share, the stake amount is reduced to fit under the limit - or the trade is skipped entirely if no room remains. This mirrors how other stake-sizing limits (such as the [available balance](#tradable-balance)) are applied, and is enforced identically in live/dry-run and backtesting.
+
+This option is unset (disabled) by default.
+
+!!! Note
+    The underlying currency of a pair is its base currency - `BTC` for `BTC/USDT`, `ETH` for `ETH/USDT`, and so on.
+
 #### Static stake amount
 
 The `stake_amount` configuration statically configures the amount of stake-currency your bot will use for each trade.
